<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-20854] lnetctl peer show prints a peer: key per peer when 0@lo is the first peer</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20854</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Problem"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Problem&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;lnetctl peer show&lt;/tt&gt; can print a &lt;tt&gt;peer:&lt;/tt&gt; key before each peer. The output then has duplicate keys at the top level. A YAML parser that rejects duplicate keys fails, for example with &lt;tt&gt;duplicate field `peer`&lt;/tt&gt;. A parser that accepts duplicate keys keeps only the last peer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="preformatted panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="preformattedContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;peer:
-     primary nid: 1.1.1.1@tcp
      ...
peer:
-     primary nid: 1.1.1.2@tcp
      ...
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Environment"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Environment&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;master (2.17.58), and every branch with the Netlink &lt;tt&gt;peer show&lt;/tt&gt; from &lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-9680" title="Improve the user land to kernel space interface for lustre" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-9680"&gt;LU-9680&lt;/a&gt; f0be00678c ("&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-9680" title="Improve the user land to kernel space interface for lustre" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-9680"&gt;LU-9680&lt;/a&gt; lnet: collect data about peer_ni by using Netlink"). The first tag with that commit is 2.15.59.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Any node where the 0@lo peer is entry 0 of the peer list.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Reproduction"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Reproduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Load libcfs with &lt;tt&gt;cpu_npartitions=1&lt;/tt&gt;, and configure LNet.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Run &lt;tt&gt;lnetctl discover 0@lo&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Run &lt;tt&gt;lnetctl peer add --prim_nid 1.1.1.1@tcp&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Run &lt;tt&gt;lnetctl peer add --prim_nid 1.1.1.2@tcp&lt;/tt&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;Run &lt;tt&gt;lnetctl peer show&lt;/tt&gt;. The output has 2 &lt;tt&gt;peer:&lt;/tt&gt; keys.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Analysis"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Analysis&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;lnet_peer_ni_show_dump()&lt;/tt&gt; sends the empty &lt;tt&gt;LNET_PEER_NI_ATTR_HDR&lt;/tt&gt; attribute only when &lt;tt&gt;idx == 1&lt;/tt&gt;. The loop increments &lt;tt&gt;idx&lt;/tt&gt; and then skips 0@lo. When 0@lo is entry 0, no message has &lt;tt&gt;idx == 1&lt;/tt&gt;, so the kernel does not send the header.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In userspace, &lt;tt&gt;yaml_parse_value_list()&lt;/tt&gt; prints the top-level key for each message. Only a received empty header clears the key. Without the header, each peer gets its own &lt;tt&gt;peer:&lt;/tt&gt; key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;tt&gt;lnet_peer_dist_show_dump()&lt;/tt&gt; has the same defect. Its only caller, &lt;tt&gt;lctl which_nid&lt;/tt&gt;, does not print the YAML, so that defect has no visible symptom.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;&lt;a name="Proposedfix"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Proposed fix&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Put the header in the first message that each dump sends, not in the message for &lt;tt&gt;idx == 1&lt;/tt&gt;. Add a sanity-lnet test that creates the 0@lo peer first on one CPT.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
